/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A shaft rotates and transmits torque.
   - The torque comes from the power: T = 60P/(2πN), with P in W, N in rpm and T in N·m.
   - At the surface of a solid shaft of diameter d, bending gives σb = 32M/(πd³) along the axis and torque gives τ = 16T/(πd³).
   - Maximum shear stress theory (Guest), used for ductile steel: τmax = √((σb/2)² + τ²) = 16√(M² + T²)/(πd³).
   - Te = √(M² + T²); τallow = 16Te/(πd³) Te is the torque that alone gives the same maximum shear stress as M and T together.
   - Maximum normal stress theory (Rankine), used for brittle material: σ1 = 16[M + √(M² + T²)]/(πd³).
   - The ASME code adds shock factors and an allowable stress.
   - An axial force P adds a direct stress σa = 4P/(πd²).
   - A hollow shaft of outer diameter do and inner diameter di, with k = di/do, has τ = 16T/(πdo³(1 − k⁴)) and σb = 32M/(πdo³(1 − k⁴)), so Te and Me work as before with d³ replaced by do³(1 − k⁴).
*/
